<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huastec people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Teenek</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Huastec, or Teenek, are an Indigenous people of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> who speak a Mayan language that separated from the other Mayan languages long ago. They live in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huasteca">Huasteca</a> region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a>. Their pre-Hispanic culture was known for shell ornaments, distinctive pottery and stone sculpture, and much of their land was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> in the 15th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huastec-people.jpg" alt="Huastec people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tania Victoria / Secretaría de Cultura de la Ciudad de México · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MX_TV_TLACUATZIN_SON_HUASTECO.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
